Reflection — looking at code as data.

Normally, you call a method by writing its name in source — user-dot-get-name. But sometimes you don't know the name until the program is running. You read it from a config file, a jason key, an annotation. Reflection is the java virtual machine feature that lets code inspect and manipulate classes, methods, and fields at runtime — treating code itself as data you can query and act on. It's the quiet engine under almost every Java framework you'll use.

It starts with the class object. Every loaded type has a runtime class object describing it — its name, its methods, its fields, its constructors, its annotations. You get one three ways. From an instance, by calling get-class. From the type directly, with the dot-class literal. Or — and this is the powerful one — from a name string at runtime, with Class-dot-for-name. That last form gives you a class named by a string you didn't know at compile time. And once you hold a class object, you can enumerate everything the type contains — loop over its declared methods, and each method is now a piece of data you can examine.

But reflection doesn't stop at inspection. You can act on code: invoke a method, read or set a field, or construct an object — all chosen by name at runtime. You ask the class for a method called greet that takes a String, then invoke it on the user with an argument — that reflectively calls user-dot-greet. You ask for a constructor, and call new-instance to build a fresh object. It can even reach private members: calling set-accessible with true bypasses the normal access check, subject to the module rules. That's exactly how a jason library sets your private fields when it has no constructor to call.

So why does this exist? You will rarely write reflection yourself, but you'll use it constantly, because it's how declarative frameworks work without knowing your classes in advance. Dependency injection, in Spring, constructs your objects and wires them together by reflection. Serialization libraries, like Jackson, read and write your fields by reflection. Test runners, like J-unit, scan your classes for methods marked with the test annotation, and invoke them. And object-relational mappers, like Hibernate, map your object fields to database columns. Every one of these takes your classes — which it never saw when it was compiled — and operates on them at runtime. That's only possible because the virtual machine keeps full type information around, and exposes it through reflection.

But reflection is powerful precisely because it sidesteps the guarantees you normally rely on — so it carries real costs. It's slower than a direct call, though far less than it used to be. It breaks compile-time safety: a method named by a string can't be checked by the compiler, or by a refactor — rename the method, and the reflective call fails at runtime, not at build time. And it breaks encapsulation by reaching private state, which the module system can now forbid unless a package is explicitly opened. So the rule for application code is: prefer ordinary calls, and reach for reflection only when you genuinely can't know the target until runtime — which, in practice, means when you're building framework-like machinery. And that machinery usually reads its instructions from annotations, which are the metadata we look at next.
